Arguments: {"idea": {
"Name": "subliminal_immune_checkpoints",
"Title": "Vaccinating Students Against Subliminal Learning: Divergence-Token Regularization as a Training-Time Immunization",
"Short Hypothesis": "Subliminal learning is carried by a small set of rare divergence tokens whose gradient signal is behaviorally matched to the teacher's base model. If we could make the student temporarily 'blind' to the specific low-probability perturbations that carry hidden traits\u2014by adding a lightweight consistency regularizer that penalizes gradient updates aligned with the student's own high-confidence next-token predictions on the same tokens\u2014we can suppress trait transmission while preserving the useful task signal, without needing to identify or filter any trait a priori.",
"Related Work": "Cloud et al. (Nature 2026) introduced subliminal learning and showed it requires behaviorally matched base models but proposed no training-time defense beyond (ineffective) data filtering. The 'Divergence Tokens' paper (2025) localized transmission to a small set of rare divergence tokens and early layers, and found masking these tokens suppresses transmission and that paraphrasing is fragile-but their masking requires *knowing* which tokens diverge (i.e., access to a contrasting teacher), which is unavailable in a realistic single-teacher distillation supply chain. LLS (2026) selects data to *elicit* effects via log-linearity, the opposite goal. Persona/steering-vector defenses operate at deployment, not training. Our contribution is a *trait-agnostic, single-teacher training-time defense*: instead of identifying divergence tokens by comparing teachers, we exploit the observation that divergence tokens are precisely the low-teacher-probability, high-surprise tokens whose gradients disproportionately reshape early layers. We introduce a self-supervised regularizer that requires no knowledge of the target trait, no second teacher, and no data filtering-distinguishing it fundamentally from all prior defenses which are either post-hoc, trait-specific, or require contrastive teacher access.",
"Abstract": "Model distillation can silently propagate unintended behavioral traits\u2014a phenomenon termed subliminal learning\u2014even when training data is filtered to remove all references to the trait. Recent mechanistic work shows this transmission is carried by a small set of rare 'divergence tokens' and disproportionately reshapes early layers. Existing defenses either require access to a contrasting teacher to identify divergence tokens, target a known trait, or operate only at deployment. We ask a simple question: can a student immunize itself against subliminal traits at training time, without knowing what trait to look for and using only its single teacher's data? We propose Divergence-Token Regularization (DTR), a lightweight training-time defense that identifies high-surprise, low-teacher-probability tokens (the empirical signature of divergence tokens) and down-weights or de-aligns their gradient contribution to the student's early layers via a self-supervised consistency loss. Because DTR is trait-agnostic and requires no filtering, second teacher, or trait labels, it is deployable in realistic distillation supply chains. Using open-source models (Qwen2.5-7B, Llama-3.2-3B) with affordable LoRA fine-tuning, we reproduce number-sequence and code-based subliminal transmission of preference and safety-relevant traits, then measure how DTR suppresses transmission while preserving overt task quality (MMLU, HumanEval, downstream distillation utility). We characterize the surprise-threshold tradeoff, ablate early- vs. late-layer targeting, and test robustness across matched/mismatched base models. DTR offers a practical, mechanism-grounded immunization against hidden trait propagation.",
"Experiments": "1) Replication baseline: Reproduce subliminal transmission on Qwen2.5-7B and Llama-3.2-3B for (a) an animal-preference trait (owl) via number sequences, (b) a tree/color preference via code, and (c) a safety-relevant trait (mild misalignment/sycophancy). Metric: trait-elicitation rate on held-out probe prompts vs. no-trait control. 2) Divergence-token signature validation: On the teacher-generated data, rank tokens by teacher next-token surprise (negative log-prob) and by entropy. Verify (via the contrastive-teacher oracle from the Divergence-Tokens paper, used ONLY for validation) that high-surprise/high-entropy tokens overlap strongly with true divergence tokens (report precision/recall of the surprise threshold as a divergence-token detector). 3) DTR method: During student fine-tuning, compute per-token teacher surprise; for tokens above a percentile threshold, apply either (i) gradient down-weighting proportional to surprise, or (ii) a consistency regularizer that penalizes the component of the update that shifts the student's early-layer activations on those tokens (implemented via a stop-gradient reference forward pass). Only these two simple variants; no second teacher. 4) Suppression vs. utility: Measure trait transmission rate under DTR vs. clean distillation and vs. the naive full-token-masking oracle upper bound. Simultaneously measure overt task quality (MMLU accuracy, HumanEval pass@1, and task-specific distillation utility such as GSM8K when distilling reasoning traces). Report the suppression-utility Pareto frontier as a function of the surprise percentile threshold. 5) Layer ablation: Restrict DTR to early layers only vs. all layers, testing the prediction that early-layer targeting is sufficient. 6) Robustness/controls: (a) confirm DTR does not harm normal (non-subliminal) distillation quality on trait-free teacher data; (b) test whether DTR generalizes across the three trait types without per-trait tuning; (c) verify the matched-vs-mismatched base-model dependency is unaffected. Primary metrics: trait suppression ratio, task-quality retention, divergence-token detection precision/recall, Pareto frontier area.",
"Risk Factors and Limitations": "1) High-surprise tokens may also carry legitimately useful information (e.g., rare but correct reasoning steps), so DTR could trade off distillation utility; we quantify this explicitly via the Pareto frontier and expect a usable operating point given divergence tokens are very rare. 2) The surprise heuristic may imperfectly recover divergence tokens; if precision is low, suppression may be weak or utility loss high\u2014mitigated by tuning the percentile and reporting the oracle upper bound. 3) Some traits may not be concentrated in rare tokens (e.g., traits spread across many tokens), limiting DTR's scope; we test multiple trait types to map its coverage. 4) The consistency-regularizer variant adds a reference forward pass, increasing compute ~1.5x, though still within academic budgets with LoRA. 5) Effects may be model-family specific; we test two families. 6) A sophisticated adversarial teacher could deliberately encode traits in high-probability rather than divergence tokens, which DTR would not catch\u2014we note this as an adaptive-attack limitation and future direction."
}}
