area.", 'Risk Factors and Limitations': "1) High-surprise tokens may also carry legitimately useful information (e.g., rare but correct reasoning steps), so DTR could trade off distillation utility; we quantify this explicitly via the Pareto frontier and expect a usable operating point given divergence tokens are very rare. 2) The surprise heuristic may imperfectly recover divergence tokens; if precision is low, suppression may be weak or utility loss high—mitigated by tuning the percentile and reporting the oracle upper bound. 3) Some traits may not be concentrated in rare tokens (e.g., traits spread across many tokens), limiting DTR's scope; we test multiple trait types to map its coverage. 4) The consistency-regularizer variant adds a reference forward pass, increasing compute ~1.5x, though still within academic budgets with LoRA. 5) Effects may be model-family specific; we test two families. 6) A sophisticated adversarial teacher could deliberately encode traits in high-probability rather than divergence tokens, which DTR would not catch—we note this as an adaptive-attack limitation and future direction."}

