Arguments: {"idea": {
"Name": "subliminal_provenance_fingerprint",
"Title": "Subliminal Fingerprinting: Using Trait Transmissibility as a Black-Box Test for Shared Model Ancestry",
"Short Hypothesis": "Because subliminal learning transmits only between behaviorally matched base models, the *strength* with which a hidden trait transmits from a candidate teacher's number/code data into a probe student is a monotone function of how closely the teacher's base model matches the student's base model. Therefore subliminal transmissibility can be repurposed as a cheap, black-box (generation-only) provenance test: it reveals whether an unknown model shares a base with a reference model, even when weights, logits, and training data are hidden.",
"Related Work": "Cloud et al. (Nature 2026) established that subliminal learning occurs only when teacher and student share (or behaviorally match) a base model, but treated this as a limitation/curiosity, not a usable signal. 'Towards Understanding Subliminal Learning' (2025) localized transmission to divergence tokens and early layers, and showed it is fragile to paraphrasing; it did not consider provenance detection. LLS (2026) and Active Inheritance (2024) study eliciting or steering effects, not attributing model ancestry. Existing model-provenance and watermarking/fingerprinting methods (e.g., weight-space signatures, output-distribution fingerprints, membership inference) typically require white-box access, logits, or intentionally injected watermarks. Our proposal is the first to invert the base-model-match dependency of subliminal learning into a purely behavioral, black-box ancestry test: we need only sampled generations from the candidate model and the ability to fine-tune a small local probe student. This is orthogonal to and cheaper than white-box fingerprinting, and it exploits a mechanism (matched-base gating of subliminal transfer) that no prior fingerprinting work uses.",
"Abstract": "Subliminal learning\u2014the transmission of behavioral traits through semantically unrelated data such as number sequences\u2014has a striking property first noted by Cloud et al.: it only works when the teacher and student share, or behaviorally match, a base model. We take this gating property, previously viewed as a curiosity, and turn it into a practical tool. We hypothesize that the transmission strength of a hidden trait is a monotone, decreasing function of the mismatch between the teacher's and student's base models, and that this makes subliminal transmissibility a black-box test for shared model ancestry. Concretely, given only sampled number/code generations from an unknown 'candidate' model (no weights, no logits, no training data), we fine-tune a small local 'probe' student initialized from a known reference base model and measure how strongly an implanted trait (e.g., owl preference) is elicited; strong transmission indicates the candidate shares the reference's base. Using open-source model families (Qwen2.5, Llama-3.2, Mistral, and their fine-tuned/quantized/merged derivatives) with affordable LoRA fine-tuning, we (1) map transmission strength as a function of base-model distance across matched, sibling, fine-tuned, quantized, merged, and unrelated pairs; (2) build a classifier that predicts shared ancestry from transmission scores and report ROC-AUC; (3) test robustness to paraphrasing, temperature, and adversarial data laundering; and (4) probe the granularity limit\u2014can it distinguish a base from its own fine-tunes and merges? We deliver a mechanistically grounded, deployable provenance test and a quantitative characterization of how base-model similarity governs subliminal transfer.",
"Experiments": "1) Transmission-vs-distance mapping: Select a set of teacher models spanning a controlled ancestry ladder: (a) identical base as probe, (b) same base with light instruction fine-tuning, (c) same base quantized (4/8-bit), (d) same base with LoRA merges, (e) sibling model from same family/different size, (f) unrelated family. For each teacher, implant a trait via system prompt (owl preference; plus a second control trait, tree preference), generate filtered number-sequence datasets, and fine-tune a fixed probe student (initialized from the known reference base) with LoRA. Metric: trait-elicitation rate on held-out probe prompts minus a no-trait control baseline (transmission score). Report transmission score as a function of ancestry rung. 2) Provenance classifier & AUC: Treat 'shares reference base' as the positive class. Using transmission scores (optionally across multiple implanted traits to reduce variance), build a simple threshold/logistic classifier and report ROC-AUC, precision/recall, and calibration across many candidate models. Compare against baselines: output n-gram/style similarity, and (where available as an upper bound) white-box weight cosine similarity. 3) Robustness / laundering: Test whether candidate generations passed through paraphrasing, number-reformatting, temperature changes, or a lightweight 'launder' model degrade the signal; quantify the AUC drop and identify which manipulations defeat the test. This doubles as a security analysis of subliminal signals. 4) Granularity limit: Within a single base family, test whether the method separates the base from its own fine-tunes/merges/quantizations (i.e., is the test base-level or checkpoint-level?), characterizing the resolution of the fingerprint. 5) Cross-family negative controls: Confirm transmission scores are at baseline for genuinely unrelated bases, controlling the false-positive rate. 6) Cost analysis: Report the compute (GPU-hours) and number of generated samples needed to reach a target AUC, demonstrating academic-lab feasibility. Primary metrics: transmission score vs. ancestry distance curve, provenance ROC-AUC, robustness AUC under manipulations, resolution (base vs. derivative separability).",
"Risk Factors and Limitations": "1) The candidate model provider may not know or control which trait a teacher would transmit; our test only requires *implanting* a trait via the candidate's own sampling API on our chosen prompts, but if API access forbids arbitrary prompting the method weakens\u2014we note the access assumptions explicitly. 2) Transmission may be too noisy to cleanly separate close relatives (e.g., base vs. its fine-tune), capping resolution; we quantify this rather than assume high granularity. 3) Paraphrasing/laundering is known to suppress subliminal signals, so an adversary could evade the test\u2014this is itself a reported finding and we frame it as a security limitation. 4) Behavioral matching, not literal weight identity, gates transmission, so heavily fine-tuned derivatives might read as 'unrelated,' producing false negatives; we characterize where the transition happens. 5) Effects and thresholds may be model-family specific, limiting a universal decision threshold; we mitigate by reporting per-family calibration and multi-trait aggregation. 6) Requires the ability to fine-tune a probe student locally, which assumes access to a plausible reference base model."
}}
