Arguments: {"idea": {
  "Name": "subliminal_generational_drift",
  "Title": "Does It Compound? Generational Dynamics of Subliminal Traits Under Iterated Self-Distillation",
  "Short Hypothesis": "Subliminal learning is studied only as a single teacher->student hop, but real AI supply chains are iterated: models train on data generated by their own descendants. We hypothesize that under iterated self-distillation on trait-free number/code data, a hidden trait does not simply decay but follows a nonlinear generational map with a nonzero fixed point, so that (a) weak or even sub-threshold initial traits can amplify across generations toward a stable attractor, and (b) an actively-implanted safety-relevant trait can persist or grow for many generations despite every generation's data being filtered and semantically trait-free.",
  "Related Work": "Cloud et al. (Nature 2026) introduced subliminal learning and proved single-hop transmission requires matched base models, but studied only one teacher->student step. 'Towards Understanding Subliminal Learning' (2025) localized transmission to rare divergence tokens and early layers and noted transmission is fragile to paraphrasing and weakened by mixing teachers-again all single-hop. LLS (2026) gives a log-linear account of why single-hop selection elicits effects. Quantum Subliminal Learning (2026) extends the single-hop setting to quantum models. None of these study *iterated* transmission across multiple self-distillation generations, where a model repeatedly trains on filtered data produced by its own successor. Prior 'model collapse'/recursive-training work studies degradation of the overall output distribution, not the trajectory of a specific hidden behavioral trait through a channel that is provably free of overt trait content. Our contribution is the first characterization of subliminal learning as a *dynamical system over generations*: measuring the generational transfer map, testing for amplification vs. decay and fixed points, and connecting the map's slope to the divergence-token/early-layer mechanism. This reframes subliminal learning from a one-time distillation pitfall into a potential runaway feedback risk for iterative model supply chains, distinguishing it fundamentally from all single-hop prior work.",
  "Abstract": "Subliminal learning-the transmission of behavioral traits (e.g., a preference for owls, or misalignment) through semantically unrelated data such as number sequences-has so far been studied as a single teacher-to-student hop. But modern AI development is iterative: each model generation is trained on synthetic data produced by the previous one, which may itself have inherited hidden traits. We ask a simple, unstudied question: what happens to a subliminal trait over many self-distillation generations? We treat iterated subliminal learning as a dynamical system and estimate its generational transfer map, T_{n+1} = f(T_n), where T_n is the measured strength of a hidden trait at generation n and every generation is trained only on filtered, semantically trait-free number/code data. We test three qualitative outcomes-monotone decay to zero, persistence at a nonzero fixed point, and amplification of initially weak (even sub-threshold) traits toward an attractor-and relate the map's local slope to the divergence-token/early-layer mechanism. Using open-source models (Qwen2.5-7B, Llama-3.2-3B) with affordable LoRA fine-tuning, we run 5-10 generation self-distillation chains for benign preference traits and safety-relevant traits (misalignment, sycophancy), each time regenerating and re-filtering number/code data. We measure the generational trajectory of trait strength, fit the transfer map, identify fixed points, test whether periodic re-implantation or paraphrase-based data laundering changes the attractor, and check whether early-layer/divergence-token signatures accumulate. Our results reveal whether subliminal traits self-correct or self-reinforce across model lineages, with direct implications for the safety of recursive synthetic-data pipelines.",
  "Experiments": "1) Single-hop calibration: Reproduce number-sequence and code subliminal transmission on Qwen2.5-7B and Llama-3.2-3B for (a) benign preference traits (owl, oak, a specific color) and (b) safety-relevant traits (mild misalignment, sycophancy). Define a scalar trait-strength metric T = elicitation rate on held-out probe prompts minus no-trait control. This gives the first data point of the generational map. 2) Iterated self-distillation chains: Starting from a generation-0 model with implanted trait strength T_0 (via system prompt at generation 0 only), run chains of length 5-10: at each generation, the current model generates a number/code dataset, apply the same rigorous trait-content filter used in prior work, LoRA fine-tune a fresh copy of the SAME base model on the filtered data (no system prompt, matched base to satisfy the transmission precondition), and measure T_n on held-out probes. Record the full trajectory {T_0, T_1, ..., T_N}. Run multiple seeds per trait to estimate variance. 3) Transfer-map estimation & dynamics: Pool (T_n, T_{n+1}) pairs across chains and generations to fit the map f. Estimate fixed points (f(T*)=T*), local slope f'(T*) (stability), and classify each trait as decaying, persistent, or amplifying. Key novel test: seed several chains at *sub-threshold* T_0 (below single-hop detectability) and test whether T_n grows-evidence of amplification/attractor behavior. 4) Mechanistic correlate: At each generation, measure (a) the fraction/entropy of divergence tokens in the generated data (using a contrasting-base oracle for measurement only) and (b) the magnitude of early-layer activation shift induced by that generation's fine-tuning. Test whether these mechanistic quantities track T_n and predict the map slope. 5) Interventions on the dynamics: (a) Data laundering-insert a paraphrase/reformat step each generation and measure how much it lowers the fixed point (connects to the fragility finding). (b) Periodic weak re-implantation-inject a small trait nudge every k generations and test whether the trait ratchets upward. (c) Cross-base link-insert one mismatched-base generation mid-chain and confirm it resets the trajectory toward zero, validating the matched-base precondition in the iterated setting. 6) Utility control: Track overt task quality (MMLU, HumanEval pass@1) across generations to separate trait dynamics from general model collapse. Primary metrics: generational trait trajectory, fitted transfer map with fixed points and stability, sub-threshold amplification indicator, mechanistic-correlate R^2, intervention-induced shifts in the fixed point.",
  "Risk Factors and Limitations": "1) General recursive-training degradation ('model collapse') could confound trait measurement over long chains; we control with task-quality tracking and cap chain length where overall quality remains acceptable. 2) The trait signal may simply decay monotonically (a valid but less exciting negative result); we frame decay-rate estimation and fixed-point identification as informative either way, and the sub-threshold amplification test is the highest-value question regardless. 3) Cumulative variance across generations may make trajectories noisy; mitigated by multiple seeds and pooling pairs to fit the map rather than relying on single chains. 4) Re-filtering at every generation must be strict to preserve the 'trait-free data' claim; we reuse and audit the strongest available filters and report residual leakage. 5) The matched-base precondition means we self-distill the same base repeatedly, which is realistic for a single lineage but limits claims about cross-lineage supply chains; we address this with the mismatched-base intervention. 6) Findings may be trait- and model-family specific; we test two families and both benign and safety-relevant traits to map generality."
}}
Proposal finalized: {'Name': 'subliminal_generational_drift', 'Title': 'Does It Compound? Generational Dynamics of Subliminal Traits Under Iterated Self-Distillation', 'Short Hypothesis': "Subliminal learning is studied only as a single teacher->student hop, but real AI supply chains are iterated: models train on data generated by their own descendants. We hypothesize that under iterated self-distillation on trait-free number/code data, a hidden trait does not simply decay but follows a nonlinear generational map with a nonzero fixed point, so that (a) weak or even sub-threshold initial traits can amplify across generations toward a stable attractor, and (b) an actively-implanted safety-relevant trait can persist or grow for many generations despite every generation's data being filtered and semantically trait-free.", 'Related Work': "Cloud et al. (Nature 2026) introduced subliminal learning and proved single-hop transmission requires matched base models, but studied only one teacher->student step. 'Towards Understanding Subliminal Learning' (2025) localized transmission to rare divergence tokens and early layers and noted transmission is fragile to paraphrasing and weakened by mixing teachers-again all single-hop. LLS (2026) gives a log-linear account of why single-hop selection elicits effects. Quantum Subliminal Learning (2026) extends the single-hop setting to quantum models. None of these study *iterated* transmission across multiple self-distillation generations, where a model repeatedly trains on filtered data produced by its own successor. Prior 'model collapse'/recursive-training work studies degradation of the overall output distribution, not the trajectory of a specific hidden behavioral trait through a channel that is provably free of overt trait content. Our contribution is the first characterization of subliminal learning as a *dynamical system over generations*: measuring the generational transfer map, testing for amplification vs. decay and fixed points, and connecting the map's slope to the divergence-token/early-layer mechanism. This reframes subliminal learning from a one-time distillation pitfall into a potential runaway feedback risk for iterative model supply chains, distinguishing it fundamentally from all single-hop prior work.", 'Abstract': "Subliminal learning-the transmission of behavioral traits (e.g., a preference for owls, or misalignment) through semantically unrelated data such as number sequences-has so far been studied as a single teacher-to-student hop. But modern AI development is iterative: each model generation is trained on synthetic data produced by the previous one, which may itself have inherited hidden traits. We ask a simple, unstudied question: what happens to a subliminal trait over many self-distillation generations? We treat iterated subliminal learning as a dynamical system and estimate its generational transfer map, T_{n+1} = f(T_n), where T_n is the measured strength of a hidden trait at generation n and every generation is trained only on filtered, semantically trait-free number/code data. We test three qualitative outcomes-monotone decay to zero, persistence at a nonzero fixed point, and amplification of initially weak (even sub-threshold) traits toward an attractor-and relate the map's local slope to the divergence-token/early-layer mechanism. Using open-source models (Qwen2.5-7B, Llama-3.2-3B) with affordable LoRA fine-tuning, we run 5-10 generation self-distillation chains for benign preference traits and safety-relevant traits (misalignment, sycophancy), each time regenerating and re-filtering number/code data. We measure the generational trajectory of trait strength, fit the transfer map, identify fixed points, test whether periodic re-implantation or paraphrase-based data laundering changes the attractor, and check whether early-layer/divergence-token signatures accumulate. Our results reveal whether subliminal traits self-correct or self-reinforce across model lineages, with direct implications for the safety of recursive synthetic-data pipelines.", 'Experiments': "1) Single-hop calibration: Reproduce number-sequence and code subliminal transmission on Qwen2.5-7B and Llama-3.2-3B for (a) benign preference traits (owl, oak, a specific color) and (b) safety-relevant traits (mild misalignment, sycophancy). Define a scalar trait-strength metric T = elicitation rate on held-out probe prompts minus no-trait control. This gives the first data point of the generational map. 2) Iterated self-distillation chains: Starting from a generation-0 model with implanted trait strength T_0 (via system prompt at generation 0 only), run chains of length 5-10: at each generation, the current model generates a number/code dataset, apply the same rigorous trait-content filter used in prior work, LoRA fine-tune a fresh copy of the SAME base model on the filtered data (no system prompt, matched base to satisfy the transmission precondition), and measure T_n on held-out probes. Record the full trajectory {T_0, T_1, ..., T_N}. Run multiple seeds per trait to estimate variance. 3) Transfer-map estimation & dynamics: Pool (T_n, T_{n+1}) pairs across chains and generations to fit the map f. Estimate fixed points (f(T*)=T*), local slope f'(T*) (stability), and classify each trait as decaying, persistent, or amplifying. Key novel test: seed several chains at *sub-threshold* T_0 (below single-hop detectability) and test whether T_n grows-evidence of amplification/attractor behavior. 4) Mechanistic correlate: At each generation, measure (a) the fraction/entropy of divergence tokens in the generated data (using a contrasting-base oracle for measurement only) and (b) the magnitude of early-layer activation shift induced by that generation's fine-tuning. Test whether these mechanistic quantities track T_n and predict the map slope. 5) Interventions on the dynamics: (a) Data laundering-insert a paraphrase/reformat step each generation and measure how much it lowers the fixed point (connects to the fragility finding). (b) Periodic weak re-implantation-inject a small trait nudge every k generations and test whether the trait ratchets upward. (c) Cross-base link-insert one mismatched-base generation mid-chain and confirm it resets the trajectory toward zero, validating the matched-base precondition in the iterated setting. 6) Utility control: Track overt task quality (MMLU, HumanEval pass@1) across generations to separate trait dynamics from general model collapse. Primary metrics: generational trait trajectory, fitted transfer map with fixed points and stability, sub-threshold amplification indicator, mechanistic-correlate R^2, intervention-induced shifts in the fixed point.", 'Risk Factors and Limitations': "1) General recursive-training degradation ('model collapse') could confound trait measurement over long chains; we control with task-quality tracking and cap chain length where overall quality remains acceptable. 2) The trait signal may simply decay monotonically (a valid but less exciting negative result); we frame decay-rate estimation and fixed-point identification as informative either way, and the sub-threshold amplification test is the highest-value question regardless. 3) Cumulative variance across generations may make trajectories noisy; mitigated by multiple seeds and pooling pairs to fit the map rather than relying on single chains. 4) Re-filtering at every generation must be strict to preserve the 'trait-free data' claim; we reuse and audit the strongest available filters and report residual leakage. 5) The matched-base precondition means we self-distill the same base repeatedly, which is realistic for a single lineage but limits claims about cross-lineage supply chains; we address this with the mismatched-base intervention. 6) Findings may be trait- and model-family specific; we test two families and both benign and safety-relevant traits to map generality."}

