Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "subliminal_channel_capacity", "Title": "How Many Bits Fit in a List of Numbers? Measuring the Information Capacity of the Subliminal Learning Channel", "Short Hypothesis": "Subliminal learning is a noisy communication channel from teacher to student carried by rare divergence tokens. We hypothesize this channel has a finite, measurable information capacity (bits of trait information transmissible per training example) that saturates: the number of distinct trait 'levels' or independent traits a student reliably recovers grows sub-linearly with dataset size and collapses under channel-noise operations (paraphrasing, filtering, temperature), following a predictable rate-distortion curve.", "Related Work": "Cloud et al. (2025) introduced subliminal learning and proved single-trait transmission requires matched base models, but treated transmission as binary (trait present/absent). 'Towards Understanding Subliminal Learning' (2025) localized transmission to a small set of rare divergence tokens and early layers and showed paraphrasing suppresses it, but never quantified how much information the channel carries or where it saturates. Classic information-capacity work (Frady et al. 2017 on superposition; DIME/CORTICAL 2021 on mutual-information channel-capacity estimation) provides the tools but has never been applied to trait transmission during distillation. No prior work frames subliminal learning as a Shannon channel, measures its bits-per-example capacity, tests whether multiple graded or multi-valued traits can be multiplexed through the same numeric data, or derives a rate-distortion curve for how channel-noise operations (paraphrasing, filtering strength, sampling temperature) degrade capacity. We turn subliminal learning from a qualitative 'trait leaks / doesn't leak' phenomenon into a quantitative communication-theoretic object, yielding both a fundamental capacity number and a principled understanding of why defenses like paraphrasing work.", "Abstract": "Subliminal learning\u2014the transmission of behavioral traits through semantically unrelated data such as number sequences\u2014is currently understood qualitatively: a trait either transfers or it does not. We reframe it as a noisy communication channel from teacher to student and ask a simple, unstudied question: how many bits of trait information can be pushed through a list of numbers? Treating the teacher's implanted trait as the channel input and the student's post-distillation elicited behavior as the channel output, we measure the mutual information between them and estimate the channel's information capacity in bits per training example. We test three predictions: (1) capacity saturates\u2014the number of distinct trait *levels* a student reliably recovers (e.g., a graded preference strength, or which of K animals) grows sub-linearly with dataset size toward a ceiling; (2) multiple independent traits can be multiplexed through the same numeric data up to an aggregate capacity budget, after which they mutually interfere; and (3) channel-noise operations (prompt paraphrasing, filtering strength, sampling temperature) trace a rate-distortion curve, quantitatively explaining why paraphrasing defeats transmission. Using open-source models (Qwen2.5-7B, Llama-3.2-3B) with affordable LoRA fine-tuning, we implant graded and multi-valued traits, generate filtered number/code datasets of varying size, estimate teacher-student mutual information with standard discriminative estimators, and fit capacity and rate-distortion curves. We connect measured capacity to the divergence-token count in the data. Our results yield the first quantitative, communication-theoretic characterization of subliminal learning and a principled account of its defenses.", "Experiments": "1) Channel setup and single-bit calibration: Reproduce number-sequence subliminal transmission on Qwen2.5-7B and Llama-3.2-3B for a binary trait (owl vs. no-owl preference). Define the channel input X (implanted trait class) and output Y (student's elicited behavior on held-out probe prompts, discretized). This gives the baseline transmission and a mutual-information estimate I(X;Y) as a starting point. 2) Multi-valued trait capacity: Extend X to K discrete classes\u2014(a) which of K animals is preferred (K=2,4,8,16), and (b) a graded preference *strength* (e.g., 5 intensity levels induced via graded system prompts). For each K, generate filtered datasets, fine-tune students (LoRA), and measure the confusion matrix between implanted class and elicited class. Estimate transmitted information I(X;Y) via the plug-in/empirical mutual information and cross-check with a discriminative MI estimator (DIME-style). Prediction: recovered information saturates as K grows, revealing a per-example capacity ceiling. 3) Rate vs. dataset size: For fixed K, vary training set size (e.g., 1k\u201350k examples) and plot recovered mutual information vs. dataset size; fit the saturation curve and estimate bits-per-example capacity. 4) Multiplexing multiple independent traits: Implant J independent traits simultaneously (animal + tree + color) via combined system prompts; measure per-trait recovered information and total transmitted information. Prediction: total information tracks an aggregate budget, with per-trait information dropping as J increases (interference beyond capacity). 5) Rate-distortion under channel noise: Apply graded channel-noise operations to the data\u2014(a) prompt/output paraphrasing at varying strengths, (b) filtering aggressiveness, (c) teacher sampling temperature\u2014and measure how recovered information decays. Fit a rate-distortion curve; quantify how paraphrasing reduces capacity, giving an information-theoretic explanation of the known fragility. 6) Mechanistic link to divergence tokens: For each condition, count/estimate divergence-token frequency in the generated data (using a contrasting-base oracle for measurement only) and regress recovered channel information against divergence-token count; test whether divergence tokens are the physical carriers whose quantity sets capacity. 7) Matched-base control: Confirm capacity collapses to zero for mismatched base models, validating the channel framing. Primary metrics: estimated mutual information I(X;Y), fitted bits-per-example channel capacity, saturation point vs. K and dataset size, per-trait information under multiplexing, rate-distortion curves, and divergence-token vs. capacity R^2.", "Risk Factors and Limitations": "1) Mutual-information estimation from finite samples is noisy and can be biased upward with large K; we mitigate by using multiple estimators (empirical plug-in with bias correction and a discriminative estimator), reporting confidence intervals, and cross-validating. 2) Graded/multi-valued traits may be hard to implant cleanly via system prompts, blurring the input alphabet; we validate teacher behavior directly before distillation and discard poorly separated levels. 3) Capacity numbers may be model-family and trait specific rather than universal; we test two families and several trait types and frame results as relative capacity curves. 4) The rate-distortion interpretation assumes paraphrasing/filtering act like memoryless noise, which may only approximately hold; we treat the curves as empirical characterizations. 5) Multiplexing interference could stem from LoRA optimization conflicts rather than channel saturation; the divergence-token regression and dataset-size controls help disentangle these. 6) Elicitation discretization choices affect I(X;Y); we report sensitivity to binning."}}
