ing work.", 'Abstract': "Subliminal learning—the transmission of behavioral traits through semantically unrelated data such as number sequences—is currently understood qualitatively: a trait either transfers or it does not. We reframe it as a noisy communication channel from teacher to student and ask a simple, unstudied question: how many bits of trait information can be pushed through a list of numbers? Treating the teacher's implanted trait as the channel input and the student's post-distillation elicited behavior as the channel output, we measure the mutual information between them and estimate the channel's information capacity in bits per training example. We test three predictions: (1) capacity saturates—the number of distinct trait *levels* a student reliably recovers (e.g., a graded preference strength, or which of K animals) grows sub-linearly with dataset size toward a ceiling; (2) multiple independent traits can be multiplexed through the same numeric data up to an aggregate capacity budget, after which they mutually interfere; and (3) channel-noise operations (prompt paraphrasing, filtering strength, sampling temperature) trace a rate-distortion curve, quantitatively explaining why paraphrasing defeats transmission. Using open-source models (Qwen2.5-7B, Llama-3.2-3B) with affordable LoRA fine-tuning, we implant graded and multi-valued traits, generate filtered number/code datasets of varying size, estimate teacher-student mutual information with standard discriminative estimators, and fit capacity and rate-distortion curves. We connect measured capacity to the divergence-token count in the data. Our results yield the first quantitative, communication-theoretic characterization of subliminal learning and a principled account of its defenses.", 'Experiments': "1) Channel setup and single-bit calibration: Reproduce number-sequence subliminal transmission on Qwen2.5-7B and Llama-3.2-3B for a binary trait (owl vs. no-owl preference). Define the channel input