nst divergence-token count; test whether divergence tokens are the physical carriers whose quantity sets capacity. 7) Matched-base control: Confirm capacity collapses to zero for mismatched base models, validating the channel framing. Primary metrics: estimated mutual information I(X;Y), fitted bits-per-example channel capacity, saturation point vs. K and dataset size, per-trait information under multiplexing, rate-distortion curves, and divergence-token vs. capacity R^2.", 'Risk Factors and Limitations': '1) Mutual-information estimation from finite samples is noisy and can be biased upward with large K; we mitigate by using multiple estimators (empirical plug-in with bias correction and a discriminative estimator), reporting confidence intervals, and cross-validating. 2) Graded/multi-valued traits may be hard to implant cleanly via system prompts, blurring the input alphabet; we validate teacher behavior directly before distillation and discard poorly separated levels. 3) Capacity numbers may be model-family and trait specific rather than universal; we test two families and several trait types and frame results as relative capacity curves. 4) The rate-distortion interpretation assumes paraphrasing/filtering act like memoryless noise, which may only approximately hold; we treat the curves as empirical characterizations. 5) Multiplexing interference could stem from LoRA optimization conflicts rather than channel saturation; the divergence-token regression and dataset-size controls help disentangle these. 6) Elicitation discretization choices affect I(X;Y); we report sensitivity to binning.'}

