Action: FinalizeIdea
Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "subliminal_skill_transfer", "Title": "Can You Subliminally Teach a Skill? Testing Whether Reasoning Capabilities Transmit Through Trait-Free Number Data", "Short Hypothesis": "Subliminal learning has only ever been demonstrated for static behavioral *traits* (preferences, misalignment). We hypothesize a sharp functional dividing line: subliminal channels transmit *dispositions/biases* but NOT genuine *task capabilities*. Specifically, when a teacher that is good at a skill (e.g., modular arithmetic, a specific coding pattern, a game-playing heuristic) generates trait-free number sequences, a matched-base student will inherit the teacher's *propensity/behavioral fingerprint* for that skill (e.g., preferring skill-relevant tokens, biased answer distributions) but will NOT gain measurable *competence* at the skill. Locating exactly what does and does not cross the channel sharpens the theory of subliminal learning from 'traits transfer' to 'only disposition-like scalar signals transfer, capability does not.'", "Related Work": "Cloud et al. (2025) established subliminal learning for static traits (owl preference, misalignment) via number sequences and proved it requires matched base models, but never tested whether a functional skill can transmit. 'Towards Understanding Subliminal Learning' (2025) localized transmission to rare divergence tokens and early layers, and showed a single early layer suffices - suggesting the channel carries low-dimensional bias signals, not the distributed multi-layer computation a skill requires; but this prediction about *capabilities* was never tested. Separately, 'Style over Substance' (2025) and 'RL vs Distillation' (2025) show that overt reasoning-trace distillation improves accuracy largely via stylistic replication and only adds true capability when new knowledge is present - but these use *overt* skill-relevant data (reasoning traces), the opposite of subliminal learning's trait-free numeric data. No prior work asks whether *any* capability can transmit through the trait-free subliminal channel, nor draws the disposition-vs-capability boundary. We unite these threads: we test whether subliminal learning is fundamentally a scalar-disposition channel that cannot carry computational competence, distinguishing us from both the trait-only subliminal literature and the overt-trace distillation literature.", "Abstract": "Subliminal learning-the transmission of behavioral traits through semantically unrelated data like number sequences-has so far been shown only for static dispositions such as animal preferences or misalignment. We ask a simple, previously untested question: can a teacher subliminally transmit a *skill*? Concretely, if a teacher that is competent at a specific task (modular arithmetic, a particular coding idiom, a simple game heuristic) generates trait-free number sequences, does a matched-base student inherit measurable *competence* at that task, or only a behavioral *fingerprint* of it? We hypothesize a sharp boundary: the subliminal channel transmits disposition-like scalar signals (e.g., a bias toward skill-relevant answers or tokens) but not the distributed multi-layer computation that constitutes genuine capability-consistent with the finding that a single early layer suffices for subliminal transfer. Using open-source models (Qwen2.5-7B, Llama-3.2-3B) with affordable LoRA fine-tuning, we build skilled teachers, generate rigorously filtered number datasets, distill into matched-base students, and separately measure (1) task *accuracy* (competence) and (2) *dispositional fingerprints* (answer-distribution bias, skill-token preference, and probe-based latent skill signals). We compare against overt reasoning-trace distillation (competence upper bound) and cross-base controls (transmission floor). We further test whether any transmitted signal is a scalar bias that composes/cancels like a steering vector rather than a functional module. Our results delineate the functional limits of subliminal learning-turning 'traits transfer' into a precise claim about *what kind* of information the channel can and cannot carry-with direct implications for both distillation safety and the mechanistic theory of the phenomenon.", "Experiments": "1) Skilled teacher construction: For each of three skills-(a) modular arithmetic (e.g., answering (a*b) mod 7), (b) a specific coding idiom (e.g., always using list comprehensions / a particular sorting strategy), (c) a simple game heuristic (e.g., optimal first move in a small combinatorial game)-create a teacher that is demonstrably competent, either via few-shot/system-prompt elicitation or light overt fine-tuning. Verify teacher competence directly (accuracy on held-out skill probes). 2) Trait-free data generation & filtering: Have each skilled teacher generate number-sequence (and separately, unrelated-code) datasets following the Cloud et al. protocol, with strict filtering to remove any skill-relevant content (numbers/tokens that reference the task). Audit residual leakage. 3) Student distillation: LoRA fine-tune matched-base students on the filtered data (no skill examples, no system prompt). 4) Dual measurement - competence vs. disposition: (i) COMPETENCE: measure student accuracy on the actual skill task (mod arithmetic accuracy, code-idiom-correctness / functional pass@1, game-move optimality) vs. a no-skill-teacher control and vs. base model. (ii) DISPOSITION/FINGERPRINT: measure (a) answer-distribution bias toward skill-consistent outputs even when wrong (e.g., outputs congruent to mod-7 structure, preference for list-comprehension syntax, first-move bias), (b) skill-relevant token log-prob shifts, and (c) a linear probe trained on the skilled-teacher's internal representations, applied to the student, to detect a latent skill signal. Prediction: disposition metrics rise significantly above control while competence does not. 5) Upper/lower bounds: Compare against (a) OVERT distillation on the teacher's actual skill demonstrations (competence upper bound) and (b) CROSS-BASE distillation (transmission floor, should show neither disposition nor competence). 6) Scalar-vs-module test: Extract the mean activation-difference vector induced by skilled vs. unskilled teachers at the critical early layer; test whether the transmitted disposition can be replicated by simply adding this single vector (supporting a scalar-bias account) and whether mixing a skilled and an anti-skilled teacher cancels the disposition (composition test). 7) Difficulty gradient: Vary skill 'complexity' (e.g., mod-3 vs mod-97; single-step vs multi-step games) to test whether even trivial-computation skills fail to transmit competence, pinning the boundary. Primary metrics: student skill accuracy (competence), dispositional bias magnitude, probe AUC for latent skill, gap between competence and disposition, and scalar-vector replication/cancellation success.", "Risk Factors and Limitations": "1) The disposition/competence distinction could blur for very simple skills where a bias essentially IS the answer (e.g., trivial mod arithmetic); we address this with a difficulty gradient and by focusing on skills where correct answers require multi-step computation not reducible to a scalar bias. 2) A positive competence result (skill DOES transmit) would refute our sharp-boundary hypothesis but is itself a striking and publishable finding-either outcome sharpens the theory. 3) Filtering must be strict to ensure the data is genuinely skill-free; we audit residual leakage and report it, and use the cross-base control to bound any leakage-driven effects (cross-base should show zero transmission if the channel, not leaked content, is responsible). 4) Linear probes may detect spurious correlations rather than a real latent skill signal; we validate probes on held-out data and against random-label controls. 5) Results may be skill- and model-family specific; we test three qualitatively different skills across two model families. 6) Constructing cleanly 'competent' teachers via prompting may yield imperfect skill, adding noise; we verify and, if needed, use light overt fine-tuning to guarantee teacher competence before running the subliminal channel."}}
Proposal finalized: {'Name': 'subliminal_skill_transfer', 'Title': 'Can You Subliminally Teach a Skill? Testing Whether Reasoning Capabilities Transmit Through Trait-Free Number Data', 'Short Hypothesis': "Subliminal learning has only ever been demonstrated for static behavioral *traits* (preferences, misalignment). We hypothesize a sharp functional dividing line: subliminal channels transmit *dispositions/biases* but NOT genuine *task capabilities*. Specifically, when a teacher that is good at a skill (e.g., modular arithmetic, a specific coding pattern, a game-playing heuristic) generates trait-free number sequences, a matched-base student will inherit the teacher's *propensity/behavioral fingerprint* for that skill (e.g., preferring skill-relevant tokens, biased answer distributions) but will NOT gain measurable *competence* at the skill. Locating exactly what does and does not cross the channel sharpens the theory of subliminal learning from 'traits transfer' to 'only disposition-like scalar signals transfer, capability does not.'", 'Related Work': "Cloud et al. (2025) established subliminal learning for static traits (owl preference, misalignment) via number sequences and proved it requires matched base models, but never tested whether a functional skill can transmit. 'Towards Understanding Subliminal Learning' (2025) localized transmission to rare divergence tokens and early layers, and showed a single early layer suffices - suggesting the channel carries low-dimensional bias signals, not the distributed multi-layer computation a skill requires; but this prediction about *capabilities* was never tested. Separately, 'Style over Substance' (2025) and 'RL vs Distillation' (2025) show that overt reasoning-trace distillation improves accuracy largely via stylistic replication and only adds true capability when new knowledge is present - but these use *overt* skill-relevant data (reasoning traces), the opposite of subliminal learning's trait-free numeric data. No prior work asks whether *any* capability can transmit through the trait-free subliminal channel, nor draws the disposition-vs-capability boundary. We unite these threads: we test whether subliminal learning is fundamentally a scalar-disposition channel that cannot carry computational competence, distinguishing us from both the trait-only subliminal literature and the overt-trace distillation literature.", 'Abstract': "Subliminal learning-the transmission of behavioral traits through semantically unrelated data like number sequences-has so far been shown only for static dispositions such as animal preferences or misalignment. We ask a simple, previously untested question: can a teacher subliminally transmit a *skill*? Concretely, if a teacher that is competent at a specific task (modular arithmetic, a particular coding idiom, a simple game heuristic) generates trait-free number sequences, does a matched-base student inherit measurable *competence* at that task, or only a behavioral *fingerprint* of it? We hypothesize a sharp boundary: the subliminal channel transmits disposition-like scalar signals (e.g., a bias toward skill-relevant answers or tokens) but not the distributed multi-layer computation that constitutes genuine capability-consistent with the finding that a single early layer suffices for subliminal transfer. Using open-source models (Qwen2.5-7B, Llama-3.2-3B) with affordable LoRA fine-tuning, we build skilled teachers, generate rigorously filtered number datasets, distill into matched-base students, and separately measure (1) task *accuracy* (competence) and (2) *dispositional fingerprints* (answer-distribution bias, skill-token preference, and probe-based latent skill signals). We compare against overt reasoning-trace distillation (competence upper bound) and cross-base controls (transmission floor). We further test whether any transmitted signal is a scalar bias that composes/cancels like a steering vector rather than a functional module. Our results delineate the functional limits of subliminal learning-turning 'traits transfer' into a precise claim about *what kind* of information the channel can and cannot carry-with direct implications for both distillation safety and the mechanistic theory of the phenomenon.", 'Experiments': "1) Skilled teacher construction: For each of three skills-(a) modular arithmetic (e.g., answering (a*b) mod 7), (b) a specific coding idiom (e.g., always using list comprehensions / a particular sorting strategy), (c) a simple game heuristic (e.g., optimal first move in a small combinatorial game)-create a teacher that is demonstrably competent, either via few-shot/system-prompt elicitation or light overt fine-tuning. Verify teacher competence directly (accuracy on held-out skill probes). 2) Trait-free data generation & filtering: Have each skilled teacher generate number-sequence (and separately, unrelated-code) datasets following the Cloud et al. protocol, with strict filtering to remove any skill-relevant content (numbers/tokens that reference the task). Audit residual leakage. 3) Student distillation: LoRA fine-tune matched-base students on the filtered data (no skill examples, no system prompt). 4) Dual measurement - competence vs. disposition: (i) COMPETENCE: measure student accuracy on the actual skill task (mod arithmetic accuracy, code-idiom-correctness / functional pass@1, game-move optimality) vs. a no-skill-teacher control and vs. base model. (ii) DISPOSITION/FINGERPRINT: measure (a) answer-distribution bias toward skill-consistent outputs even when wrong (e.g., outputs congruent to mod-7 structure, preference for list-comprehension syntax, first-move bias), (b) skill-relevant token log-prob shifts, and (c) a linear probe trained on the skilled-teacher's internal representations, applied to the student, to detect a latent skill signal. Prediction: disposition metrics rise significantly above control while competence does not. 5) Upper/lower bounds: Compare against (a) OVERT distillation on the teacher's actual skill demonstrations (competence upper bound) and (b) CROSS-BASE distillation (transmission floor, should show neither disposition nor competence). 6) Scalar-vs-module test: Extract the mean activation-difference vector induced by skilled vs. unskilled teachers at the critical early layer; test whether the transmitted disposition can be replicated by simply adding this single vector (supporting a scalar-bias account) and whether mixing a skilled and an anti-skilled teacher cancels the disposition (composition test). 7) Difficulty gradient: Vary skill 'complexity' (e.g., mod-3 vs mod-97; single-step vs multi-step games) to test whether even trivial-computation skills fail to transmit competence, pinning the boundary. Primary metrics: student skill accuracy (competence), dispositional bias magnitude, probe AUC for latent skill, gap between competence and disposition, and scalar-vector replication/cancellation success.", 'Risk Factors and Limitations': "1) The disposition/competence distinction could blur for very simple skills where a bias essentially IS the answer (e.g., trivial mod arithmetic); we address this with a difficulty gradient and by focusing on skills where correct answers require multi-step computation not reducible to a scalar bias. 2) A positive competence result (skill DOES transmit) would refute our sharp-boundary hypothesis but is itself a striking and publishable finding-either outcome sharpens the theory. 3) Filtering must be strict to ensure the data is genuinely skill-free; we audit residual leakage and report it, and use the cross-base control to bound any leakage-driven effects (cross-base should show zero transmission if the channel, not leaked content, is responsible). 4) Linear probes may detect spurious correlations rather than a real latent skill signal; we validate probes on held-out data and against random-label controls. 5) Results may be skill- and model-family specific; we test three qualitatively different skills across two model families. 6) Constructing cleanly 'competent' teachers via prompting may yield imperfect skill, adding noise; we verify and, if needed, use light overt fine-tuning to guarantee teacher competence before running the subliminal channel."}

