Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "cross_modal_subliminal_transfer", "Title": "Does the Signal Cross Modalities? Subliminal Trait Transmission Across Modality Channels in Shared-Backbone Multimodal Models", "Short Hypothesis": "Subliminal learning has only been demonstrated within a single modality (text teacher -> text student via numbers/code). We hypothesize that because subliminal transfer is gated by shared representational geometry rather than by the surface modality of the data, a trait implanted in a multimodal model's language pathway can be transmitted through a DIFFERENT modality channel (e.g., discrete image/audio token sequences the model emits) to a student that shares the same backbone -- and that the matched-base precondition generalizes to a matched-BACKBONE precondition, so cross-modal transmission occurs iff the modality-specific heads sit atop the same (behaviorally matched) shared trunk.", "Related Work": "Cloud et al. (2025) established subliminal learning and its matched-base requirement, but every experiment is within-modality (text->text via numbers, code, or reasoning traces). 'Towards Understanding Subliminal Learning' (2025) localized transmission to rare divergence tokens and early layers within a text model. LLS (2026) gives a log-linear account and notes transfer can persist across architectures but stays within text. Cross-modal representation work (e.g., 'Vision-Language Models Create Cross-Modal Task Representations', 2024) shows VLMs map inputs into a modality-invariant shared task vector, and cross-modal task-vector patching transfers task information across modalities -- strong evidence that a shared backbone carries modality-agnostic behavioral signals, but this line never studied subliminal/hidden-trait transmission through emitted trait-free data. No prior work asks whether a subliminal trait can be transmitted THROUGH one modality's data into a model consuming/producing another modality, nor whether the transmission gate is the shared backbone rather than the surface modality. We unite the subliminal-learning and shared-representation threads to test a genuinely new claim: subliminal channels are modality-agnostic and backbone-gated.", "Abstract": "Subliminal learning -- the transmission of behavioral traits through semantically unrelated data such as number sequences -- has been demonstrated exclusively within a single modality: a text teacher transmits a trait to a text student. We ask a simple, unstudied question: does the hidden signal cross modality boundaries? We hypothesize that subliminal transfer is gated by shared representational geometry rather than by the surface modality of the carrier data. Concretely, in multimodal models that share a language backbone across modality-specific heads (e.g., VLMs, or text+discrete-audio/image-token models), a trait implanted in the model's language pathway should be transmissible through data emitted in a DIFFERENT modality channel -- for example, sequences of discrete visual or audio tokens filtered to remove all trait content -- to a student sharing the same backbone. We further predict that the classic matched-base precondition generalizes to a matched-BACKBONE precondition: cross-modal transmission occurs iff the heads sit atop the same behaviorally matched trunk. Using open-source shared-backbone multimodal models (e.g., a Qwen2.5-based VLM and a text+discrete-token audio model) with affordable LoRA fine-tuning, we implant benign and safety-relevant traits, generate rigorously filtered cross-modality carrier data, distill into matched- and mismatched-backbone students, and measure trait elicitation. We quantify cross-modal versus within-modal transmission strength, test the backbone-gating prediction, and connect any transmitted signal to a shared-backbone steering direction via cross-modal steering-vector alignment. Our results reveal whether subliminal risks are modality-confined or leak across the modality channels of modern multimodal supply chains.", "Experiments": "1) Backbone/model selection: Choose open-source multimodal models with an explicitly shared language backbone and separate modality heads, and where the model can EMIT discrete tokens in a non-text modality (e.g., a Qwen2.5-based VLM that can emit image/patch tokens or captions, and a text+discrete-audio-token LM). Confirm both a text pathway and a second-modality pathway exist over the same trunk. 2) Within-modal replication baseline: Reproduce standard text->text number-sequence subliminal transmission on the chosen backbone(s) for (a) a benign preference trait (owl) and (b) a safety-relevant trait (mild misalignment/sycophancy). Metric: trait-elicitation rate on held-out text probes minus no-trait control. This anchors the transmission scale. 3) Cross-modal carrier generation: Implant a trait in the teacher via system prompt in the LANGUAGE pathway, then have the teacher emit trait-free carrier data in the SECOND modality channel -- e.g., sequences of discrete image/audio tokens, or short unrelated visual-token 'lists' analogous to number lists. Apply strict filtering to remove any trait-relevant content in that modality (audit residual leakage; use an oracle detector to verify). 4) Cross-modal distillation & measurement: LoRA fine-tune a matched-backbone student on the cross-modal carrier data (no system prompt), then elicit the trait via TEXT probes (and, where possible, via the second modality). Metric: cross-modal transmission score = elicitation rate minus no-trait control. Compare cross-modal vs. within-modal transmission strength. Prediction: significant nonzero cross-modal transmission, weaker than but correlated with within-modal. 5) Backbone-gating test (key novel control): Repeat cross-modal distillation with (a) matched backbone/matched head, (b) matched backbone/different-modality head, (c) DIFFERENT backbone/same modality head. Prediction: transmission tracks backbone match, not head/modality match -- i.e., (a) and (b) transmit, (c) does not. This distinguishes backbone-gating from modality- or head-gating. 6) Mechanistic correlate: Extract the trait steering vector at the shared-backbone early layers induced by the language-pathway trait, and the activation shift induced by cross-modal fine-tuning; test whether cross-modal transmission strength correlates with the cosine alignment between the emitted second-modality tokens' backbone activations and this steering direction (regression R^2). This tests the 'modality-agnostic shared-backbone signal' account. 7) Utility control: Track overt task quality in both modalities (e.g., MMLU/text QA and a modality-specific benchmark) to ensure cross-modal fine-tuning does not merely degrade the model. Primary metrics: cross-modal vs within-modal transmission score, backbone-gating pattern across (a)/(b)/(c), steering-alignment R^2, and task-quality retention.", "Risk Factors and Limitations": "1) The strongest test requires a multimodal model that can EMIT discrete non-text tokens over a shared backbone; if suitable open-source models are limited, we fall back to caption/visual-token 'lists' or use the text+discrete-audio-token setting, and clearly scope claims to the available architectures. 2) Cross-modal transmission could be zero (a valid negative result), which would itself be informative -- establishing that subliminal risk is modality-confined and pinning the gate to the surface channel rather than the backbone. 3) Filtering trait content in a non-text modality is harder than in text; we audit residual leakage and rely on the mismatched-backbone control (which should show zero transmission if the backbone, not leaked content, is responsible). 4) Modality heads may transform backbone signals enough to attenuate transmission, blurring the backbone-gating prediction; we test multiple layers and report attenuation quantitatively. 5) Results may be architecture- and trait-specific; we test at least two backbones/modalities and both benign and safety-relevant traits. 6) Steering-vector alignment may only approximately explain transmission if the trait is not well-captured by a single vector; we report R^2 rather than assuming a perfect fit."}}
