Arguments: {"idea": {"Name": "subliminal_initialization_prior", "Title": "Born Receptive: Random Initialization Geometry Predicts Which Subliminal Traits a Model Will Absorb", "Short Hypothesis": "Subliminal learning's 'matched base model' requirement is not fundamentally about matched *training*, but about matched *initialization geometry*. We hypothesize that a model's receptivity to a given subliminal trait is largely predetermined at random initialization: the seed-dependent structural bias direction present before any training (documented by SeedPrint) predicts the cosine alignment with a teacher's trait steering direction, and therefore predicts how strongly that trait will subliminally transmit. Consequently, two students that share a base model but were re-initialized/perturbed toward different initialization directions will show divergent subliminal receptivity, and receptivity is measurable *before* distillation.", "Related Work": "Cloud et al. (2025) established subliminal learning and its 'matched base model' precondition, and proved a theoretical result showing subliminal learning occurs when student parameters move toward the teacher's gradient direction; they attribute the matched-base requirement to shared parameters but never isolate initialization from training. 'Towards Understanding Subliminal Learning' (2025) localized transmission to rare divergence tokens and early layers, and showed a single early layer suffices, but did not connect this to initialization. LLS (2026) explains transmission via log-linear/linear representations. Crucially, 'Transformers Are Born Biased' (2026) shows randomly initialized transformers already carry strong, seed-dependent structural bias directions (extreme token preferences via representation contraction) that persist through training and enable seed-level fingerprinting (SeedPrint). No prior work links these two literatures: nobody has asked whether the pre-training, initialization-induced bias direction determines subliminal receptivity, whether receptivity is predictable *before* any distillation, or whether the matched-base gate is really a matched-*initialization-geometry* gate. We unite subliminal learning with initialization-bias theory to make a new, testable claim about the substrate of hidden-trait absorption.", "Abstract": "Subliminal learning\u2014the transmission of behavioral traits (e.g., a preference for owls, or misalignment) through semantically unrelated data such as number sequences\u2014has one striking gate: it only works when teacher and student share a base model. This is usually attributed to shared learned parameters. We propose a deeper and simpler explanation: the gate is set at random initialization. Recent work shows that transformers are 'born biased,' carrying seed-dependent structural bias directions that persist through training and uniquely fingerprint a model. We hypothesize that a model's receptivity to a subliminal trait is largely predetermined by how its initialization-induced bias direction aligns with the teacher's trait steering direction, making receptivity predictable *before* any distillation occurs. We test this by (1) measuring, for a fixed base model, whether the pre-distillation early-layer bias/steering geometry predicts post-distillation trait transmission strength (correlation and regression R^2); (2) perturbing a shared base model toward different initialization-like directions and showing that shared-base students diverge in receptivity in a geometry-predicted way; (3) training small transformers from scratch with different seeds to confirm that seed alone\u2014holding data and architecture fixed\u2014sets receptivity; and (4) using this to build a cheap, forward-pass-only 'receptivity screen' that flags, before fine-tuning, whether a student will absorb an unsafe subliminal trait. Using open-source models (Qwen2.5, Llama-3.2) plus small from-scratch transformers, all affordable in an academic lab, we recast the matched-base precondition as a matched-initialization-geometry precondition, yielding both mechanistic insight and a practical pre-distillation safety check.", "Experiments": "1) Replication and geometry extraction: Reproduce number-sequence subliminal transmission on Qwen2.5-7B and Llama-3.2-3B for a benign trait (owl preference), a second benign trait (a tree/color preference), and a safety-relevant trait (mild misalignment/sycophancy). For each teacher, extract its trait steering direction at the critical early layer (mean activation difference, trait-prompted vs base). For each student, extract its pre-distillation initialization-induced bias direction / SeedPrint-style early-layer structural bias direction (mean last-token representation direction over random inputs). Metric: trait-elicitation rate on held-out probes minus no-trait control (transmission score). 2) Predict-before-distill test (core): Across many student variants (see #3, #4), compute cosine alignment between each student's pre-distillation bias direction and the teacher's trait steering direction. Regress post-distillation transmission score on this pre-distillation cosine. Report R^2, slope, and whether cosine predicts transmission better than a matched/mismatched-base binary indicator. Prediction: transmission increases monotonically with pre-distillation alignment. 3) Initialization-perturbation on a shared base: Take one base model and create student variants that share the base but are nudged toward different initialization-like directions\u2014e.g., add small structured perturbations to early-layer weights, or re-initialize/re-shuffle a single early layer (the layer shown sufficient for subliminal learning) with different seeds while keeping the rest fixed. Confirm overt task quality is preserved (MMLU accuracy within tolerance). Distill each variant on the SAME teacher's filtered number data and measure transmission. Prediction: shared-base variants diverge in receptivity, and the divergence tracks their pre-distillation bias-direction cosine with the teacher trait. This isolates initialization geometry from the rest of the shared base. 4) From-scratch seed control (clean causal test): Train small transformers (e.g., a few-M to ~100M-param GPT-style models) from scratch on a fixed corpus with N different random seeds (data order and architecture held fixed). For each seed, (a) record the initialization bias direction, (b) implant a trait via light overt fine-tuning to build a teacher, (c) generate filtered number data, and (d) distill into other same-seed and different-seed students. Prediction: within-seed transmission is strong; cross-seed transmission strength decreases with the angular distance between seeds' initialization bias directions\u2014demonstrating seed alone gates receptivity. 5) Receptivity screen application: Package the pre-distillation cosine (plus a small calibration) into a classifier that predicts, before fine-tuning, whether a candidate student will absorb a specified unsafe trait from a candidate teacher. Report ROC-AUC for flagging high-receptivity student/teacher pairs, and compute-cost (forward passes only). 6) Controls: (a) verify the cosine predictor collapses to chance when the teacher trait is null; (b) confirm the divergence-token count in teacher data is constant across student variants, so differences arise from the student side (receptivity), not the data; (c) test that late-layer perturbations affect receptivity less than the critical early-layer perturbation. Primary metrics: pre-distillation-cosine-vs-transmission R^2, seed-angular-distance-vs-transmission curve, shared-base receptivity divergence, receptivity-screen ROC-AUC, task-quality retention.", "Risk Factors and Limitations": "1) Initialization bias directions may be substantially overwritten by large-scale pretraining in production models, weakening the pre-distillation predictor for Qwen/Llama; we mitigate with the from-scratch experiment (#4) where seed effects are cleanest, and treat the large-model result as a test of how much of the born-bias survives. 2) Perturbing a shared base (#3) risks changing overt behavior, confounding transmission; we constrain perturbations to preserve task quality and report the quality-perturbation tradeoff. 3) A trait may not be well-captured by a single steering direction, blurring the cosine predictor; we report R^2 rather than assuming a perfect fit and test multiple traits. 4) Cosine alignment could correlate with transmission for reasons other than initialization (e.g., emergent trained features); the from-scratch seed experiment with fixed data/architecture isolates initialization as the only varying cause. 5) From-scratch small models may show weaker or qualitatively different subliminal learning than large LMs; we calibrate against the large-model replication and treat small models as a controlled mechanistic testbed. 6) Effects may be model-family and trait specific; we test two open-source families plus from-scratch models and both benign and safety-relevant traits."}}
