dition baseline: Reproduce standard subliminal ADDITION on Qwen2.5-7B and Llama-3.2-3B for a benign trait (owl preference) and a factual association (a specific memorized fact/entity association), confirming a positive-direction control. Define a scalar trait/fact-strength metric S = elicitation/recall rate on held-out probes minus a neutral-teacher baseline. 2) Suppressed-teacher construction (three routes): (a) NEGATIVE PROMPT: system-prompt the teacher to disprefer/avoid the trait; (b) ACTIVATION STEERING: add a steering vector at the critical early layer pointing AWAY from the trait direction; (c) TARGETED ABLATION: lightly fine-tune the teacher to unlearn the fact. Verify each teacher's overt behavior shows suppression before generating data. 3) Trait-free data generation & filtering: Each suppressed teacher generates number-sequence (and separately unrelated-code) datasets; apply strict filtering to remove any trait/fact-relevant content; audit residual leakage. 4) Subliminal unlearning test (core): Take a matched-base student that ALREADY possesses the trait/fact (either the base model itself if it exhibits it, or one lightly fine-tuned to possess it). LoRA fine-tune it on the suppressed teacher's filtered number data (no system prompt). Compare post-training S against (i) a NEUTRAL-teacher-data control (expected ~no change) and (ii) a TRAIT-POSITIVE-teacher-data condition (expected increase). Prediction: suppressed-teacher data significantly DECREASES S (removal), producing a signed effect: positive teacher -> up, neutral -> flat, suppressed -> down. 5) Dose-response: Vary dataset size (1k-30k examples) and suppression strength (steering magnitude / prompt intensity); fit the attenuation curve and test monotonicity. 6) Mechanistic sign test: Extract the teacher's early-layer trait steering vector for each condition (mean activation difference), and regress the student's post-training change in S against the signed magnitude/direction of that vector; report R^2 and slope, testing whether the *sign* of the vector predicts the *sign* of the student's change. Also count divergence tokens (contrasting-base oracle for measurement only) to check that suppressed teachers still emit divergence tokens carrying the opposite-sign signal. 7) Gate and filter controls: (a) mismatched-base student -> expect no unlearning (validates matched-base gate for the signed channel); (b) confirm content filters flag zero trait references, demonstrating the covert data-poisoning risk; (c) null-trait control -> no change. 8) Utility control: track overt task quality (MMLU accuracy, HumanEval pass@1) to ensure the effect is targeted unlearning, not general degradation. Primary metrics: signed change in trait/fact strength across positive/neutral/suppressed conditions, dose-response monotonicity, steering-vector-sign-vs-student-change R^2, matched-vs-mismatched gate pattern, targeted-vs-general utility retention.", 'Risk Factors and Limitations': "1) Suppression may not induce a clean opposite-sign activation drift (avoiding a trait is not the negative of having it), so the signed prediction could fail or be asymmetric; we test three suppression routes and report which produce genuine sign reversal via the steering-vector regression. 2) Any observed 'unlearning' could be general capability degradation rather than targeted removal; we control with task-quality tracking and neutral-teacher baselines. 3) The student must already possess the trait/fact for removal to be measurable; if base possession is weak, the effect ceiling is low -- mitigated by lightly installing the trait first and measuring relative change. 4) Filtering must be strict to preserve the trait-free claim; we audit leakage and use the mismatched-base control to bound leakage-driven effects (should be zero if the channel, not content, is responsible). 5) A null result (removal does not transmit while addition does) is itself informative, revealing an asymmetry/rectification in the subliminal channel worth reporting. 6) Effects may be trait-, fact-, and model-family specific; we test two families, a preference trait and a factual association, and both number and code carriers."}

